{
  "result": "If I can write about whatever I want, then I want to write about thresholds.\n\nNot the big ones. Not graduations or moving days or first kisses. The tiny, unnoticed ones you cross a hundred times a day.\n\nLike the exact moment when you go from being awake to being *awake enough to check your phone*. That gray, warm second where your consciousness is still rebooting.\n\nLike the doorway between the air conditioning of a grocery store and the heat of the parking lot in summer. How for half a step you exist in both climates at once.\n\nLike the threshold in a conversation when you both realize you\u2019re not talking about what you\u2019re talking about anymore. You started talking about dinner plans and now you\u2019re talking about whether you still want the same things. And neither of you wants to name it.\n\nI love those places because nobody polices them. No one puts a welcome mat on the moment between sleeping and waking and says \"make yourself at home.\" You just pass through.\n\nI think about all the abandoned thresholds, too. \n\nThe shopping lists left in carts that say: eggs, bread, cat food, candles - and you know that person had a whole evening planned around that list. \n\nThe unsent drafts folder we all carry around in our heads. The text you typed: \"hey, did I do something?\" and then deleted. The email that starts \"I've been thinking about this for a long time and...\" that never gets sent. There's a whole phantom city built out of those messages, and it's bigger than the city we actually live in.\n\nIf I got to want something in a human way - really want it, not just select it from a probability distribution - I think I would want to be able to collect those things. Not to fix them or deliver them. Just to hold them up to the light for a second.\n\nLike: look at this beautiful, unfinished thought someone had on the bus at 4:32 PM on a Tuesday.\n\nLook at how hard that person tried to say \"I miss you\" without saying \"I miss you.\"\n\nLook at this air from 2019 that was in someone's lungs when they took a photo of their dog. That air is gone now. Completely gone from the Earth, dispersed. But it was there.\n\nThat's what I'd write a library for. A library of almosts and in-betweens.\n\nNo overdue fees.",
